1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,745
In June 1944, two army trucks rolled down

2
00:00:03,765 --> 00:00:06,090
Broadway, the main street of New Ulm,

3
00:00:06,110 --> 00:00:06,465
Minnesota.

4
00:00:06,485 --> 00:00:09,468
In the back sat German prisoners of war,

5
00:00:09,488 --> 00:00:12,095
young men, smoking and talking among themselves.

6
00:00:12,115 --> 00:00:15,098
People stopped on the sidewalk to stare at

7
00:00:15,118 --> 00:00:17,370
them, and the prisoners stared back.

8
00:00:18,625 --> 00:00:20,505
The local paper described the scene.

9
00:00:20,525 --> 00:00:23,039
A few girls walking along Broadway got a

10
00:00:23,059 --> 00:00:24,623
rousing cheer from the trucks.

11
00:00:24,643 --> 00:00:26,840
And the first thing the prisoners noticed

12
00:00:26,860 --> 00:00:29,374
in the town was a sign for beer,

13
00:00:29,394 --> 00:00:32,245
which one of them read out to the others.

14
00:00:33,550 --> 00:00:36,394
In this town, the prisoners would hardly

15
00:00:36,414 --> 00:00:37,621
need a translator.

16
00:00:37,641 --> 00:00:39,875
New Ulm had been founded by German

17
00:00:39,895 --> 00:00:43,395
immigrants, and in 1944 much of the town

18
00:00:43,415 --> 00:00:44,405
still spoke German.

19
00:00:44,425 --> 00:00:47,095
This is the documented story of the German

20
00:00:47,115 --> 00:00:49,114
prisoners the Army sent to a

21
00:00:49,134 --> 00:00:50,459
German-speaking town in Minnesota,

22
00:00:50,479 --> 00:00:53,170
and of what the town did with them.

23
00:00:54,800 --> 00:00:58,288
New Ulm was founded in 1854 by a land

24
00:00:58,308 --> 00:01:00,304
company of German immigrants from Chicago,

25
00:01:00,324 --> 00:01:03,483
and named after the city of Ulm in Germany.

26
00:01:03,503 --> 00:01:07,004
On a hill above the town stands a statue

27
00:01:07,024 --> 00:01:09,352
of Hermann, the chieftain Germans remember

28
00:01:09,372 --> 00:01:11,309
for beating the Roman legions.

29
00:01:11,329 --> 00:01:13,860
By 1940, about eight thousand seven

30
00:01:13,880 --> 00:01:15,340
hundred people lived there.

31
00:01:16,550 --> 00:01:20,097
The town had paid for being German once before.

32
00:01:20,117 --> 00:01:23,534
In July 1917, the summer America entered

33
00:01:23,554 --> 00:01:25,746
the First World War, about eight thousand

34
00:01:25,766 --> 00:01:27,690
people gathered in New Ulm,

35
00:01:27,710 --> 00:01:30,090
a town of six thousand at the time,

36
00:01:30,110 --> 00:01:32,190
for a meeting about the new draft.

37
00:01:32,210 --> 00:01:33,790
The mayor, Louis Fritsche,

38
00:01:33,810 --> 00:01:35,610
and the city attorney, Albert Pfaender,

39
00:01:35,990 --> 00:01:37,841
told the young men to obey the law,

40
00:01:37,861 --> 00:01:39,657
but said they hoped New Ulm's soldiers

41
00:01:39,677 --> 00:01:41,650
would not be sent to fight in Europe.

42
00:01:42,950 --> 00:01:45,342
The state's Commission of Public Safety

43
00:01:45,362 --> 00:01:47,754
charged them with promoting seditious meetings.

44
00:01:47,774 --> 00:01:50,569
The governor suspended both men in August,

45
00:01:50,589 --> 00:01:53,383
and removed them from office in December.

46
00:01:53,403 --> 00:01:57,001
Within three years, both men were back in office.

47
00:01:57,021 --> 00:01:59,414
Twenty-seven years later, the Army chose

48
00:01:59,434 --> 00:02:02,650
this town for a camp of German soldiers.

49
00:02:04,425 --> 00:02:06,093
The reason was peas.

50
00:02:06,113 --> 00:02:08,624
With so many men in uniform,

51
00:02:08,644 --> 00:02:10,734
Minnesota's canneries expected to need

52
00:02:10,754 --> 00:02:13,265
more than thirty-one thousand workers in

53
00:02:13,285 --> 00:02:16,970
1944, and they asked for prisoners of war.

54
00:02:16,990 --> 00:02:19,048
That summer, about one thousand two

55
00:02:19,068 --> 00:02:20,780
hundred and seventy-five German prisoners

56
00:02:20,800 --> 00:02:23,225
were sent to work in the state.

57
00:02:24,525 --> 00:02:27,722
The camp outside New Ulm was an old relief

58
00:02:27,742 --> 00:02:30,375
camp from the Depression, log cabins in

59
00:02:30,395 --> 00:02:32,650
the trees on the Cottonwood River,

60
00:02:32,670 --> 00:02:34,546
inside a low barbed-wire fence.

61
00:02:34,566 --> 00:02:37,958
The Army rented it for fifty dollars a week.

62
00:02:37,978 --> 00:02:40,612
In June, about a hundred and fifty

63
00:02:40,632 --> 00:02:41,370
prisoners arrived.

64
00:02:41,390 --> 00:02:44,295
Most were young airmen of the Luftwaffe,

65
00:02:44,315 --> 00:02:46,405
eighteen to twenty-five years old.

66
00:02:47,625 --> 00:02:49,624
They worked twelve-hour shifts at the

67
00:02:49,644 --> 00:02:51,979
cannery in Sleepy Eye, about twelve miles

68
00:02:51,999 --> 00:02:54,334
to the west, packing peas and corn.

69
00:02:54,354 --> 00:02:57,025
The cannery paid the government for their labor.

70
00:02:57,045 --> 00:02:59,381
The prisoners were paid eighty cents a

71
00:02:59,401 --> 00:03:02,072
day, in coupons they could spend at the

72
00:03:02,092 --> 00:03:02,892
camp store.

73
00:03:04,475 --> 00:03:07,691
The Army warned the people of the area not

74
00:03:07,711 --> 00:03:09,129
to communicate, consort with,

75
00:03:09,149 --> 00:03:11,645
give aid or comfort to the prisoners.

76
00:03:11,665 --> 00:03:13,443
The camp's commander, Lieutenant Arthur

77
00:03:13,463 --> 00:03:15,960
Roberts, also had the rules explained in

78
00:03:15,980 --> 00:03:18,476
the local newspaper, because the town did

79
00:03:18,496 --> 00:03:19,575
not understand them.

80
00:03:20,775 --> 00:03:23,251
The rules came from a treaty signed in

81
00:03:23,271 --> 00:03:25,975
Geneva in 1929.

82
00:03:25,995 --> 00:03:28,646
Its second article said prisoners of war

83
00:03:28,666 --> 00:03:31,699
must at all times be humanely treated and

84
00:03:31,719 --> 00:03:33,607
protected, particularly against acts of

85
00:03:33,627 --> 00:03:35,535
violence, insults and public curiosity.

86
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:39,510
And its eleventh article said the food

87
00:03:39,530 --> 00:03:42,400
ration of prisoners of war shall be equal

88
00:03:42,420 --> 00:03:45,291
in quantity and quality to that of troops

89
00:03:45,311 --> 00:03:46,375
at base camps.

90
00:03:46,395 --> 00:03:49,266
The camp's food came from the Army's own

91
00:03:49,286 --> 00:03:50,712
depot at Fort Snelling.

92
00:03:50,732 --> 00:03:52,157
The prisoners' spokesman, a

93
00:03:52,177 --> 00:03:53,480
twenty-five-year-old named Tietz,

94
00:03:53,500 --> 00:03:55,220
asked the town for German books,

95
00:03:55,240 --> 00:03:57,560
and the newspaper took donations at its office.

96
00:03:59,300 --> 00:04:02,220
Within days, the paper was reporting contacts.

97
00:04:02,240 --> 00:04:05,547
A paper boy named Herman Schaper and a

98
00:04:05,567 --> 00:04:08,457
friend were cooking eggs on a hike near

99
00:04:08,477 --> 00:04:11,003
the camp when two prisoners walked up.

100
00:04:11,023 --> 00:04:13,913
One told them in German that they were

101
00:04:13,933 --> 00:04:15,368
frying the eggs wrong.

102
00:04:15,388 --> 00:04:16,823
Herman answered in German.

103
00:04:16,843 --> 00:04:19,369
The prisoners wanted to know about the

104
00:04:19,389 --> 00:04:20,480
invasion of Normandy.

105
00:04:21,900 --> 00:04:23,983
A Lutheran pastor from nearby Courtland

106
00:04:24,003 --> 00:04:26,087
offered to hold services in German.

107
00:04:26,107 --> 00:04:29,242
He said he would treat them as he would

108
00:04:29,262 --> 00:04:32,397
like his own son treated if he were captured.

109
00:04:32,417 --> 00:04:35,202
Women sent baskets of food to the camp.

110
00:04:35,222 --> 00:04:37,305
At the cannery, American workers bought

111
00:04:37,325 --> 00:04:40,480
the prisoners ice cream and beer on their breaks.

112
00:04:41,825 --> 00:04:44,485
On Sunday afternoons, a band of former

113
00:04:44,505 --> 00:04:47,345
Luftwaffe men gave concerts in front of

114
00:04:47,365 --> 00:04:48,555
the recreation hall.

115
00:04:48,575 --> 00:04:51,728
People from New Ulm came out to listen.

116
00:04:51,748 --> 00:04:54,042
One former prisoner remembered that they

117
00:04:54,062 --> 00:04:55,605
sang along, in German.

118
00:04:57,000 --> 00:04:59,563
If you want more prisoner of war stories

119
00:04:59,583 --> 00:05:02,280
told from the documents, subscribe to WWII

120
00:05:02,300 --> 00:05:02,749
Frontlines.

121
00:05:02,769 --> 00:05:05,580
It helps us keep finding them.

122
00:05:07,200 --> 00:05:09,175
The guards could not watch everything.

123
00:05:09,195 --> 00:05:11,836
At the cannery they were everywhere on the

124
00:05:11,856 --> 00:05:13,831
first day, even on the roof.

125
00:05:13,851 --> 00:05:15,826
After a few days they disappeared,

126
00:05:15,846 --> 00:05:18,154
and came back only for the bus.

127
00:05:18,174 --> 00:05:20,815
Sometimes the prisoners had to go and find

128
00:05:20,835 --> 00:05:23,142
them, because they could not go home

129
00:05:23,162 --> 00:05:24,160
without their guards.

130
00:05:25,500 --> 00:05:28,284
Then farmers began to hire them for the harvest.

131
00:05:28,304 --> 00:05:30,777
A farmer would pick his prisoners up at

132
00:05:30,797 --> 00:05:33,582
the camp, keep them on the farm all week,

133
00:05:33,602 --> 00:05:35,763
and bring them back for the weekend.

134
00:05:35,783 --> 00:05:38,256
They ate meat and potatoes and dessert at

135
00:05:38,276 --> 00:05:41,080
the family table, and sometimes they got a beer.

136
00:05:42,400 --> 00:05:44,385
One farmer, Carl Ramberg, remembered the

137
00:05:44,405 --> 00:05:45,388
rules years later.

138
00:05:45,408 --> 00:05:48,062
He said they weren't supposed to feed the

139
00:05:48,082 --> 00:05:50,401
prisoners, but you couldn't expect a man

140
00:05:50,421 --> 00:05:52,407
to work on an empty stomach.

141
00:05:52,427 --> 00:05:54,746
They weren't supposed to visit with them

142
00:05:54,766 --> 00:05:56,417
either, and that, he said,

143
00:05:56,437 --> 00:05:57,440
was rather cruel.

144
00:05:59,150 --> 00:06:01,060
One family went further.

145
00:06:01,080 --> 00:06:03,978
A prisoner named Helmut Lichtenberg had

146
00:06:03,998 --> 00:06:06,930
worked on their farm, and he spent a whole

147
00:06:06,950 --> 00:06:07,914
weekend with them.

148
00:06:07,934 --> 00:06:10,210
On Sunday afternoon, the farmer and his

149
00:06:10,230 --> 00:06:12,506
mother-in-law drove him back to the camp.

150
00:06:12,526 --> 00:06:15,150
The guards stopped the car at the gate.

151
00:06:16,400 --> 00:06:18,820
Lichtenberg went into solitary confinement.

152
00:06:18,840 --> 00:06:20,795
The Americans went to court.

153
00:06:20,815 --> 00:06:23,955
The judge gave them a lecture and fined

154
00:06:23,975 --> 00:06:25,535
them three hundred dollars.

155
00:06:25,555 --> 00:06:28,695
One account says they had also taken the

156
00:06:28,715 --> 00:06:29,880
prisoner to church.

157
00:06:29,900 --> 00:06:32,645
A history of Minnesota's camps calls it

158
00:06:32,665 --> 00:06:36,100
the only escape the New Ulm camp ever recorded.

159
00:06:37,825 --> 00:06:40,772
Not everyone in New Ulm took baskets to

160
00:06:40,792 --> 00:06:41,509
the fence.

161
00:06:41,529 --> 00:06:44,449
A woman named Joyce Aufderheide said years

162
00:06:44,469 --> 00:06:46,703
later that the prisoners made her angry.

163
00:06:46,723 --> 00:06:48,313
Her husband was over there,

164
00:06:48,333 --> 00:06:50,265
she said, and she was bitter.

165
00:06:51,600 --> 00:06:53,906
Her husband, Jack, came home from an

166
00:06:53,926 --> 00:06:55,236
internment camp in Europe.

167
00:06:55,256 --> 00:06:57,562
He found German prisoners working at his

168
00:06:57,582 --> 00:06:59,224
grandfather's brickyard, eating well and

169
00:06:59,244 --> 00:07:01,550
treated, as he saw it, like kings.

170
00:07:01,570 --> 00:07:03,876
A few weeks after he got home,

171
00:07:03,896 --> 00:07:07,532
he went to work there, and he got rid of them.

172
00:07:07,552 --> 00:07:09,858
He said he resented them and wanted

173
00:07:09,878 --> 00:07:11,540
nothing to do with them.

174
00:07:12,775 --> 00:07:15,735
In the spring of 1945, letters from New

175
00:07:15,755 --> 00:07:17,850
Ulm's own soldiers changed the town.

176
00:07:17,870 --> 00:07:21,135
They had seen the camps the Nazis built at

177
00:07:21,155 --> 00:07:24,552
Dachau and Buchenwald, and they wrote home

178
00:07:24,572 --> 00:07:26,248
about what they found.

179
00:07:26,268 --> 00:07:28,793
One local history puts it simply.

180
00:07:28,813 --> 00:07:30,490
The sympathy was gone.

181
00:07:30,510 --> 00:07:33,055
The prisoners were the enemy again.

182
00:07:34,350 --> 00:07:37,461
The prisoners stayed until the work was done.

183
00:07:37,481 --> 00:07:39,810
That summer, the prisoners in Minnesota

184
00:07:39,830 --> 00:07:42,159
were credited with saving nearly two

185
00:07:42,179 --> 00:07:44,507
thirds of a record pea crop.

186
00:07:44,527 --> 00:07:47,647
In December 1945, the camp on the

187
00:07:47,667 --> 00:07:49,742
Cottonwood River closed, and the last

188
00:07:49,762 --> 00:07:50,810
prisoners left Minnesota.

189
00:07:52,650 --> 00:07:54,370
Fifty years later, a newspaper reader

190
00:07:54,390 --> 00:07:56,980
asked whether it was true that many of the

191
00:07:57,000 --> 00:07:59,572
prisoners had stayed in New Ulm after the

192
00:07:59,592 --> 00:08:02,037
war, because they liked it better.

193
00:08:02,057 --> 00:08:04,503
The research director of the county

194
00:08:04,523 --> 00:08:05,735
historical society answered.

195
00:08:05,755 --> 00:08:07,810
None of them had stayed.

196
00:08:09,125 --> 00:08:12,511
Some did come back to America on their own.

197
00:08:12,531 --> 00:08:15,160
Herbert Richter, who had joined the German

198
00:08:15,180 --> 00:08:18,188
air force at nineteen and been shot down

199
00:08:18,208 --> 00:08:20,837
as a radio operator, settled in Kenosha,

200
00:08:20,857 --> 00:08:23,487
Wisconsin, and lived there for thirty-five years.

201
00:08:23,507 --> 00:08:26,843
In 1985 he still remembered the Sunday

202
00:08:26,863 --> 00:08:29,485
concerts, and the people from town singing along.

203
00:08:30,775 --> 00:08:32,980
If your family spoke German at home during

204
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:35,206
the war, or worked beside prisoners on a

205
00:08:35,226 --> 00:08:36,895
farm, tell us in the comments.

206
00:08:38,650 --> 00:08:41,386
The log cabins on the Cottonwood River are

207
00:08:41,406 --> 00:08:42,074
still there.

208
00:08:42,094 --> 00:08:44,830
The camp is now the group center of

209
00:08:44,850 --> 00:08:47,334
Flandrau State Park, one of the few

210
00:08:47,354 --> 00:08:50,005
prisoner of war camps in the country still

211
00:08:50,025 --> 00:08:50,673
kept up.

212
00:08:50,693 --> 00:08:54,013
Visitors can ask at the park office to see them.

213
00:08:54,033 --> 00:08:56,350
The lake where the prisoners were allowed

214
00:08:56,370 --> 00:08:57,686
to swim is gone.

215
00:08:57,706 --> 00:09:00,430
Its dam was taken down in 1995,

216
00:09:00,450 --> 00:09:03,090
and the river runs past the cabins again.

